.TH ADATE 1 "Oct 27, 2026" "" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
adate \- print the date in the Coptic calendar
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adate
.RI [ YYYY-MM-DD ]
.br
.B adate
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B adate
converts a Gregorian calendar date into its equivalent date in the
Coptic calendar and prints the result to standard output.
.PP
If invoked with no arguments,
.B adate
uses the current local date. If invoked with a single argument in the
form
.IR YYYY-MM-DD ,
that date is converted instead.
.PP
The Coptic date is calculated via an intermediate Modified Julian Day
representation, and the output consists of the day number followed by
the name of the Coptic month, e.g. \(dq15 Thout\(dq. The Coptic
calendar has twelve months of thirty days each, plus a short
intercalary thirteenth month, \(dqPi Kogi Enavot\(dq, of five or six
days, the sixth being the Coptic leap day.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a short usage message and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I YYYY-MM-DD
A Gregorian calendar date to convert, given as a four-digit year, a
two-digit month, and a two-digit day, separated by hyphens (for
example,
.IR 2026-10-27 ).
If this argument cannot be parsed as a valid date, an error message is
printed to standard error, followed by a usage message, and the
program exits with a non-zero or otherwise unspecified status.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B adate
exits with status 0 on success. If more than one argument is supplied,
a usage message is printed. If the supplied date argument cannot be
parsed, an error is reported and a usage message is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Print today's date in the Coptic calendar:
.RS
.B adate
.RE
.TP
Convert a specific Gregorian date:
.RS
.B adate 2026-10-27
.RE
.TP
Show usage information:
.RS
.B adate \-\-help
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
The
.B adate
program was written by Martin Keegan.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR date (1)
